NextKey — the browser writes, the command line opens
====================================================
2026-09-06, Sepolia · ENSv2 hackathon deployment

What this evidences: a secret encrypted in a web browser, granted to a
recipient who never registered with NextKey, written on chain to a
second-level .eth name registered the same afternoon, and then decrypted from
a laptop by a completely separate implementation.

Nothing in the two halves shares code beyond the wrapping rule, and nothing
in either half was told where the other would put things. They agree because
they follow the same rule, not because they were introduced.

The phrase below was generated by try.html's throwaway generator. It is a
valid BIP-39 mnemonic that has never held a coin, and the page refuses to
write anything on chain until the visitor confirms as much.


1 · A name that was not ours this morning
─────────────────────────────────────────
The ENS manager app has been unreliable through this hackathon, so the
registrar was driven directly — commit–reveal, paid in the deployment's own
mock USDC.

  registrar     0x7d1b7f586a62ac3f54b9a396849757814283270b
  name          nextkeydemo.eth
  owner         0x9780aFE81EF5b58333c83e05d5C07797BD81dd0B
  price         8.000021 USDC (mock)
  expires       2027-09-06

  approve       0x0c38e209d5c989b29152e3079be6dac7c2d4c94d8a60979ef58a75de88ef1f67  success
  commit        0xb1f8d873bab7ff2d54c024c53aa28ce5efc42ab53e1ef23e13dfe796abdf9167  success
  register      0xc64731caee7700bf8068d0097b25307016f1343cb92f12f216ee1d8467ada982  success

Registration leaves the resolver at the zero address deliberately: the
commitment is computed over it, so it cannot be changed between commit and
register.


2 · Its own resolver
────────────────────
The first attempt attached the deployment's publicResolverV2. It refused every
write, and the refusal was silent — see FEEDBACK-ENS.md finding 9. A
Permissioned Resolver deployed for this name works.

  factory       0x894bc9cc8ff1ad96b8a288c86a8c71d662c07780
  deploy        0x0654b313dca0f7adcc7b65bb7d8821195a219d1b563be85e410f1b7dda6ddf1b  success
  resolver      0xfD8847ba5d2fFE2d4AEe83df8052287c99f2E046

  attach        0x5260c622e00a5f7cecdac2c7847a9772cfdd08b26133932facb1abaf19ddea5f  success
                setResolver on the .eth registry 0x1d78834d…3971e — not ours

Checked before spending anything further, with scripts/probe-name.mjs:

  registry      0xfD8847ba5d2fFE2d4AEe83df8052287c99f2E046
  found by UR   0xfD8847ba5d2fFE2d4AEe83df8052287c99f2E046   ← the two agree
  name owner    0x9780aFE81EF5b58333c83e05d5C07797BD81dd0B

  ✓ setText(bytes name, string, string)
  ✗ setText(bytes32 node, string, string)   empty revert — no such function


3 · The browser encrypts and writes
───────────────────────────────────
nextkey.li/try.html, steps 1 to 3 and 6. No NextKey server is involved at any
point; the page is static files on shared hosting.

  secret        a throwaway phrase from the page's own BIP-39 generator
  recipient     anna.nextkey.eth
                her X25519 public key read live from HER OWN record. She was
                not asked, and holds nothing of ours.
  grant record  nextkey.grant.e8d67b995039bdcf
                the first 16 hex of sha256(her public key), computed in the
                browser — no directory, no lookup service

Both writes were simulated before either signature was requested, so a failure
on the second could not leave a ciphertext on chain that nobody can open.

  nextkey.secret               0x5273c330e10b0dafde178294…
  nextkey.grant.e8d67b99…      0x1427979333287379901ad907…

Confirmed independently in the ENS explorer: nextkeydemo.eth, Records 2,
resolver 0xfD88…E046, owner 0x9780…dd0B.


4 · The command line opens it
─────────────────────────────
Different machine, different language runtime, different implementation of the
same wrapping rule, and a name the tool had never heard of.

  $ node --env-file=.env scripts/nextkey.mjs open nextkeydemo.eth anna

    nextkeydemo.eth  opened as anna
    woman tribe gym rubber kitten outside pool sniff slender enemy include fiber


What this rules out
───────────────────
It is not a mock. The browser's construction is web/src/nk-crypto.mjs and the
laptop's is scripts/nextkey-core.mjs; web/test/interop.mjs checks the two
against each other on synthetic data, and this run checks them across a public
chain on data neither side chose.

It is not our name. nextkeydemo.eth sits under .eth, not under nextkey.eth,
and its resolver was deployed for it alone.

It is not our server. The page that wrote these records is static HTML on
Cyon. Take it offline and the records stay, and anna can still open them.

It is not a privileged path. The grant was addressed by fingerprinting a key
published by someone who has never interacted with NextKey. Any name carrying
a nextkey.pubkey record works the same way.

And it is not something we can read. The content key is wrapped to anna's
public key; her private half is on her machine and appears nowhere in this
log, in the chain, or in any of our code.


What it does not show
─────────────────────
Revocation on this name. Clearing the grant record requires the setter role,
which the name's owner holds — but the run above stopped at writing.
Revocation and expiry are evidenced separately in revocation.log and
expiry.log, on nextkey.eth subnames.
